---
// The home page. The first screen follows the tracker pattern's section 1.3 (ruled by John
// 2026-10-16), in order: the eyebrow, the thing held (the computed year in figures, with its
// spread and the two numbers beside it), the question as the page title, one paragraph for a
// reader who has never heard of the project, the last-checked strip, two buttons. Below it: what
// a treatment is, the eight branches with live counts, the kinds of record with live counts, and
// who is asking. Written for a reader who has never heard of any of this (design standard, section
// 1a); UK spelling throughout. Every number comes from records.json (scripts/export.py), which
// reads the computed headline from data/headline.json; nothing here is typed in. The site's own
// skin and its trunk object, the crossing chart, come from the design session and are not here.
// The first-screen order and the strip are copied from The Belt Equation's home page through The
// Upgrade Tree's (tracker pattern, section 7).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { records, longDate, plural, percent, percentWords, spreadWords, yearWords, REPO } from '../lib/records';

const c = records.counts;
const h = records.headline;
const reading = records.reading;
const branches = records.branches;
const fixed = records.fixed_dates;
const loneMover = reading && reading.movers.length === 1 ? reading.movers[0] : null;
---

<Base>
  <section class="home-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">A public tracker of the race between repair and ageing</p>
    {h.available ? (
      <p class="big-number">{h.median_year}</p>
    ) : (
      <p class="big-number pending">Not computed yet</p>
    )}
    <p class="big-claim-line">
      {h.available
        ? <>The middle estimate of the year medicine starts adding more than a year of healthy life for every year that passes, <span class="spread">and {spreadWords()}.</span></>
        : <>The year medicine starts adding more than a year of healthy life for every year that passes, worked out from the record and never chosen by hand, will stand here.</>}
    </p>
    {h.available && (
      <div class="beside-row" aria-label="The two numbers beside the year">
        <div>
          <span class="stat">{percent(h.before_2050)}</span>
          <span class="stat-label">the chance it arrives before {fixed.before}</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="stat">{percent(h.after_2100)}</span>
          <span class="stat-label">the share of runs in which it does not arrive this century</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    )}
    <h1 class="question">When do we start outrunning ageing?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every year you live, medicine gets a little better. Right now it does not get better fast enough: for a person in middle age, the years they can expect to have left fall by about a year every year, less the small gains new treatments add. This site asks when, if ever, that stops being true: when repair overtakes ageing, so that someone who keeps up with the best available care finds their remaining years holding steady or rising instead of falling. It breaks that question into eight kinds of damage that would have to be dealt with, lists the specific treatments and measuring tools that would have to arrive under each, and computes a year from them. Beside the year it keeps a dated record of who promised what and when, held against what has actually been shown.
    </p>
    <p class="scan-strip">
      <span class="strip-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span>
        {reading
          ? <>Last checked {longDate(reading.read_on)}: {plural(reading.read, 'test read', 'tests read')}, {reading.moved.length === 0 ? 'none moved' : reading.moved.length === 1 ? 'one moved' : `${reading.moved.length} moved`}{loneMover ? <>: <a href={loneMover.path}>{loneMover.name}</a></> : ''}. <a href={reading.path}>See the week's reading.</a></>
          : <>No reading of the week's news against the treatments has run yet. The first one follows the site's launch, and this line will say when it ran and what came of it. <a href="/reading/">About the weekly reading.</a></>}
      </span>
    </p>
    <p class="ctas">
      <a class="btn" href="/branches/">What has to happen</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/method/#who">Who is asking</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section explain narrow">
    <p>
      A <em>treatment</em>, here, is one specific thing that would have to arrive for the year to move: a drug, a procedure, a gene therapy, or a measurement a regulator accepts as proof that something slowed ageing. Each one is written down with a plain test of what would count, written so that two readers would agree; who or what settles that test; where it stands today on a five-rung ladder from idea only to in wide use, with the date and the public record for every rung reached; and the project's estimate of the year it reaches the clinic, as a range with the reasoning beside it. The eight <em>branches</em> are the eight things that would have to be dealt with: seven kinds of damage that build up in a body over a lifetime, and the tools that would show whether any repair worked.
    </p>
    {h.available ? (
      <p>
        None of the three numbers at the top is typed in. The tree of {plural(c.leaves, 'treatment and tool', 'treatments and tools')} is played out {h.method.runs.toLocaleString('en-GB')} times, drawing a year for each from its range; the middle of all those runs is the year at the top, and the two numbers beside it are read off the same spread. The middle nine tenths of the runs land {spreadWords()}. Every estimate is still a first pass, written on {longDate(h.computed_on)} and waiting on an outside review; the year will move as the ranges are argued, and every move is dated with its reason. <a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out.</a>
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>{h.reason} <a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out.</a></p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shead"><h2>The eight branches</h2><span>what has to be dealt with, and what is recorded under each</span></div>
    <ul class="branches">
      {branches.map((b) => (
        <li>
          <a class="bcard" href={b.path}>
            <div class="bcard-top"><span class="n">{b.order} of {branches.length}</span><span>{b.leaves === 0 ? 'nothing yet' : plural(b.leaves, 'treatment or tool', 'treatments and tools')}</span></div>
            <div class="bcard-title">{b.name}</div>
            <div class="bcard-sub">{b.meaning}</div>
            <div class="bcard-foot">
              {b.furthest_label ? `Furthest stage: ${b.furthest_label.toLowerCase()}` : 'No stage reached'}
              {b.median_handled_year !== null && <>. Handled, in the middle run, by <span class="year">{yearWords(b.median_handled_year)}</span></>}
            </div>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="dim" style="font-size: 14px; margin-top: 12px;">
      A branch counts as handled in the year its second treatment reaches the clinic, so a branch with nothing in the clinic pushes the year out and one where a regulator accepts a measure of ageing as proof pulls it in. <a href="/branches/">The tree</a> has the branches one at a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow">
    <div class="shead"><h2>What is recorded</h2><span>six kinds of record, each with its own page</span></div>
    <ul class="records">
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/branches/">The treatments and tools</a></h3>
        <p>One page each, under its branch, with the test, who settles it, the stage reached and the estimate of when it reaches the clinic. {c.siblings} are shared with the sister site The Upgrade Tree, and the two records point at each other.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.leaves, 'written so far', 'written so far')}, {c.leaves_in_clinic} already in the clinic</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/claims/">Who promised what</a></h3>
        <p>A dated log of what named people and companies have said about when, each with the primary source and what it commits to, and a verdict filled in as the dates pass. It sits beside the year and moves no number.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.claims, 'claim', 'claims')} by {records.claims_counts.speakers} people and organisations</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/self-experiments/">Other people's public self-experiments</a></h3>
        <p>Who, what they do, what they measure, what they claim, and what the public record actually shows. A claim about one person is a story, not a record, and it moves nothing on the tree.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.experiments, 'written so far', 'written so far')}</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/story/">The story so far</a></h3>
        <p>Every paper, trial report and regulator's record that bears on the tree, dated, each saying what it settled or moved, and the ones that moved nothing beside them with equal billing.</p>
        <span class="count">{plural(c.publications, 'publication logged', 'publications logged')}</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/reading/">This week's reading</a></h3>
        <p>Every treatment and tool read against the week's news, with a verdict for each, including the weeks when nothing moved. The weeks when nothing moved are the point.</p>
        <span class="count">{c.scans_real === 0 ? 'none has run yet; the first follows the launch' : plural(c.scans_real, 'reading so far', 'readings so far')}</span>
      </li>
      <li class="record-card">
        <h3><a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out</a></h3>
        <p>The model in plain words, every setting with its reason and its strongest alternative, one run written out by hand, and the working documents published as written.</p>
        <span class="count">{h.available ? `computed ${longDate(h.computed_on)}` : 'not computed yet'}</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose" id="who">
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0.4em;">Who is asking</h2>
    <p>
      John Fredrickson, who also keeps <a href="https://beltequation.com" rel="noopener">The Belt Equation</a>, <a href="https://thequietgalaxy.com" rel="noopener">The Quiet Galaxy</a> and <a href="https://theupgradetree.com" rel="noopener">The Upgrade Tree</a>, three sister sites built the same way: one question at the top, a small fixed set of reasons under it, and under each reason the specific things that would have to happen, each with a test, each watched and dated. This one is the fourth. It records what has been shown and what has been promised; it recommends nothing, and nothing on it is medical advice. The author's own health record is not part of it. The place to push back is <a href="/disagree/">Disagree with something</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="status-line">
      Records as of {longDate(records.exported_on)}. Everything on this site is built from a public repository, <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">on GitHub</a>, where every number carries its source and every change its date. {h.available ? `The year was computed on ${longDate(h.computed_on)} and the chance before ${fixed.before} is ${percentWords(h.before_2050)}.` : ''}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
